{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,60p' supabase/functions/_shared/server/knowledge.ts; sed -n '195,228p' supabase/migrations/20261010040000_normal_forms.sql; sed -n '1,115p' ANSWER.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,60p' supabase/functions/_shared/server/knowledge.ts; sed -n '195,228p' supabase/migrations/20261010040000_normal_forms.sql; sed -n '1,115p' ANSWER.md\"","aggregated_output":"// Knowledge base storage: SourceDocuments from the sources pipeline\n// (core/sources/run.ts) → documents + document_chunks, then embeddings.\n// Idempotent: each document carries a hash of what produced its chunks, so a\n// re-run skips unchanged documents entirely and keeps embeddings of unchanged\n// chunks of a changed one. A privacy scan drops chunks with emails or\n// secret-like strings.\n//\n// Which sources exist is not configured per project any more\n// (projects.knowledge_sources was the one-off seed path): connectors bring\n// published work, GitHub brings the project folder, the landing crawl brings\n// the site. Documents only the seed produced are marked source = 'seed'.\n\nimport { chunkMarkdown, cleanMarkdown } from \"../draft/chunk.ts\";\nimport type { SourceDocument } from \"../core/types.ts\";\nimport { admin } from \"./ctx.ts\";\nimport { embedder, toVector } from \"./models.ts\";\n\nconst EMAIL = /[A-Za-z0-9._%+-]+@[A-Za-z0-9-]+\\.[A-Za-z]{2,}/g;\nconst SECRETISH = /(AKIA[0-9A-Z]{16}|sk-[A-Za-z0-9]{20,}|ghp_[A-Za-z0-9]{20,}|aws ssm|get-parameter|BEGIN [A-Z ]*PRIVATE KEY)/i;\n/** Pure. Why a chunk must not be stored (emails, secret-like strings); [] = fine. */\nexport function privacyFlags(text: string): string[] {\n  const f: string[] = [];\n  const emails = (text.match(EMAIL) ?? []).filter((e) => !/@\\d/.test(e) && !/^(io\\.github|worklore)@/.test(e));\n  if (emails.length) f.push(\"email\");\n  if (SECRETISH.test(text)) f.push(\"secret-like\");\n  return f;\n}\n\nexport async function sha256(s: string) {\n  const h = await crypto.subtle.digest(\"SHA-256\", new TextEncoder().encode(s));\n  return [...new Uint8Array(h)].map((b) => b.toString(16).padStart(2, \"0\")).join(\"\");\n}\n\n/** The hash of everything that shapes a document's chunks and rows. */\nexport const docSha = (d: SourceDocument) =>\n  sha256([d.kind, d.title, d.published_at, d.label ?? \"quotable\", d.lang ?? \"en\", d.markdown].join(\"\\n\\u0000\"));\n\nexport interface ExistingDoc { url: string; content_sha256: string | null; source: string | null }\nexport interface IncomingDoc { url: string; sha: string; source: string }\n\n/**\n * Pure. What a run must do per document: `write` new or changed ones (chunks\n * replaced), only `relabel` an unchanged one whose source differs (e.g. a seed\n * document the pipeline now reproduces byte for byte), leave the rest.\n */\nexport function planDocs(existing: ExistingDoc[], incoming: IncomingDoc[]) {\n  const old = new Map(existing.map((e) => [e.url, e]));\n  const seen = new Set<string>();\n  const write: string[] = [], relabel: string[] = [], unchanged: string[] = [];\n  for (const d of incoming) {\n    if (seen.has(d.url)) continue; // first source wins within a run\n    seen.add(d.url);\n    const e = old.get(d.url);\n    if (!e || e.content_sha256 !== d.sha) write.push(d.url);\n    else if (e.source !== d.source) relabel.push(d.url);\n    else unchanged.push(d.url);\n  }\n  return { write, relabel, unchanged };\n}\n\nalter table drafts\n  add column tokens_in int generated always as\n    ((tokens->>'input')::int + coalesce((tokens->>'cache_read')::int, 0) + coalesce((tokens->>'cache_write')::int, 0)) stored,\n  add column tokens_out int generated always as ((tokens->>'output')::int) stored;\n\n-- ------------------------------------------------------------------ derived: people.first/last_touch_at\n-- Kept as a cache of min/max(touches.occurred_at), now by the database for\n-- every writer (the collector did read-then-write; issue touches didn't update\n-- it at all). greatest/least on insert is safe under concurrent inserts.\ncreate function touches_person_span() returns trigger language plpgsql security definer set search_path = public as $$\nbegin\n  if tg_op = 'INSERT' then\n    update people set first_touch_at = least(first_touch_at, new.occurred_at),\n                      last_touch_at = greatest(last_touch_at, new.occurred_at)\n    where id = new.person_id\n      and (first_touch_at is null or last_touch_at is null or new.occurred_at < first_touch_at or new.occurred_at > last_touch_at);\n  else\n    update people p set first_touch_at = s.lo, last_touch_at = s.hi\n    from (select x.id, (select min(occurred_at) from touches where person_id = x.id) lo,\n                       (select max(occurred_at) from touches where person_id = x.id) hi\n          from (select old.person_id as id union select new.person_id) x where x.id is not null) s\n    where p.id = s.id;\n  end if;\n  return null;\nend $$;\ncreate trigger touches_person_span after insert or delete or update of person_id, occurred_at on touches\n  for each row execute function touches_person_span();\nrevoke execute on function touches_person_span() from public, anon, authenticated;\n\n-- The decision RPC no longer sets last_touch_at itself: its touch does.\ncreate or replace function record_draft_outcome(\n  p_draft_id uuid,\n  p_outcome draft_outcome,\n  p_final_text text default null,\n# What is not in 3NF?\n\nThis audits the **final schema after all migrations**, not just the initial `CREATE TABLE` statements. A primary key on `id` does not make a table 3NF when another column determines a third column. Database-enforced consistency reduces the risk of a copy, but does not remove the functional dependency that makes it redundant.\n\n## Clear remaining dependencies\n\n| Table and columns | Dependency and consequence | Evidence / sensible change |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `model_calls`: `usage`, `tokens_in`, `tokens_out`, `cost_usd` | For a call with a usage breakdown, `tokens_in = input + cache_read + cache_write`, `tokens_out = output`, and `cost_usd` is also present in `usage`. These are separately writable copies. They can disagree, including in the spend view, which sums the scalar columns. | [Migration](supabase/migrations/20261015010000_ask_code_model_calls.sql) lines 28–31, 45–52; [writer](supabase/functions/_shared/server/usage.ts) lines 64–68 and [ask-code writer](supabase/functions/_shared/server/ask_code.ts) lines 87–90. Keep one representation and derive the other, or enforce a single database writer if the breakdown is optional. Preserve `NULL` for unknown cost rather than treating it as zero. |\n| `code_questions`: `usage`, `cost_usd` | The completed run stores cost both as `usage.cost_usd` and as `cost_usd`; the error path separately computes `cost_usd` from `model_calls`. No constraint guarantees agreement. The run's `usage` totals are also a materialized aggregate of `model_calls` rows identified by `run_id`. | [Migration](supabase/migrations/20261015010000_ask_code_model_calls.sql) lines 56–78; [success and error writers](supabase/functions/_shared/server/ask_code.ts) lines 95–106. Derive a displayed cost from the chosen authoritative usage source, or let a database-controlled aggregate maintain a cache. |\n| `voice_samples`: `quality`, `scores` | For model-judged samples, `scores.quality` duplicates `quality`; both are written by the application and can diverge. Rule-rejected samples have `quality = 0` and `scores = NULL`, so a replacement expression must preserve that case. | [Migration](supabase/migrations/20261010010000_sources_voice_knowledge.sql) lines 25–27; [verdict construction](supabase/functions/_shared/core/sources/voice.ts) lines 93–106 and [write](supabase/functions/_shared/core/sources/run.ts) lines 133–137. Store the score once; expose the other shape in a view or generate it with explicit rule-rejection semantics. |\n\n## Intentional redundancy, still outside strict 3NF\n\n- **Child `project_id` copies.** For example, `document_chunks.document_id → documents.project_id`, yet `document_chunks.project_id` is stored too. The same dependency exists in `identities` through `person_id`, `items` through `person_id` (and `account_id` when present), `touches` through `person_id`/`item_id`, `idea_items` through either parent, and `project_memory`/`documents` through a non-null `theme_id`. The [normal-forms migration](supabase/migrations/20261010040000_normal_forms.sql) lines 23–71 explicitly retains these for RLS and indexes and adds composite foreign keys. Thus these are formal redundancies, **but the listed composite FKs prevent conflicting project IDs**. `items.platform` likewise copies `accounts.platform` for account-backed items and is checked by the composite FK; accountless discovery items have their own platform.\n- **Cached aggregates.** `ideas.asked_by` is determined by linked `idea_items` and their `items.person_id`; `people.first_touch_at` and `last_touch_at` are determined by `touches`. The [normal-forms migration](supabase/migrations/20261010040000_normal_forms.sql) lines 77–95 and 201–221 installs recount/span triggers. These are deliberate derived caches, not independently owned facts. The triggers cover ordinary writes, though the schema alone does not prove that pre-existing data matches them or prevent a privileged direct update to a cache.\n- **Content hashes.** `documents.content_sha256` and `document_chunks.content_sha256` are application-written functions of source/chunk content, used to skip work and reuse embeddings. See the [knowledge writer](supabase/functions/_shared/server/knowledge.ts) lines 65–107. There is no database expression or trigger checking them; an independent update to content or hash can make the cache stale. `voice_samples.text_sha256` is similar, but its normalization uses the server's JavaScript whitespace rule and the [migration](supabase/migrations/20261010040000_normal_forms.sql) lines 15–18 explicitly documents why it was kept. Compute hashes in one database writer if exact database enforcement is needed, or treat them as application-maintained caches and audit them.\n\n## What the earlier migration already fixed\n\nDo **not** count these as current findings: `idea_items.person_id` was dropped (the original partial-key/2NF problem); `document_chunks.kind`, `source_url`, `source_ref`, and `label` were dropped in favor of the parent document; `documents.is_public`, `drafts.tokens_in/out`, `items.triage_*`, and `projects.url_key` are generated; `plans.allowed_models` became `plan_models`. See [normal-forms migration](supabase/migrations/20261010040000_normal_forms.sql) lines 73–198 and 276–341, plus the later [safe token expression](supabase/migrations/20261011120000_tokens_generated_safe.sql).\n\nArrays and JSON are **not automatically** 1NF or 3NF violations in Postgres. The old `plans.allowed_models` was relational data queried as membership and has been normalized. Current arrays such as project languages/topics and draft checks need separate child tables only if the app needs independently identifiable, constrained, or joined elements. Similarly, `code_questions.repo` and `commit_sha` describe the repository and revision used *for that answer*; treating `repo` as a historical snapshot avoids falsely calling it a copy of the project's current repository.\n\nThis is a **static audit** of migrations and writers. No live database was provided, so I cannot claim a count of inconsistent rows. Before changing these columns, run read-only mismatch counts on the deployed schema and decide which representation is authoritative; then test the migration and all writers together.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
